OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Sharp-one-dimensional-Lieb-Thirring-inequalities-for-matrix-potentials-October-5-2026", "title": "Sharp one-dimensional Lieb–Thirring inequalities for matrix potentials", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Sharp-one-dimensional-Lieb-Thirring-inequalities-for-matrix-potentials-October-5-2026/sharp-matrix-lieb-thirring.pdf", "family": "262", "family_title": "Sharp finite-matrix Lieb–Thirring inequalities and all equality cases", "abstract_html": "We prove the sharp one-dimensional Lieb–Thirring inequality for every finite matrix size and every exponent \\(1/2\\lt \\gamma\\lt 3/2\\). The optimal constant is the scalar one-bound-state constant, independently of the matrix size. The inequality bounds the full sum of negative eigenvalue moments for every measurable Hermitian positive semidefinite potential <i>W</i> satisfying \\(\\int_\\mathbb R\\mathop{\\mathrm{tr}}\\nolimits (W^{\\gamma+1/2})\\lt \\infty\\). The matrices may have arbitrary rank and need not commute at different points.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Sharp matrix inequality", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\operatorname{Tr}(H_W)^\\gamma_- \\leq C_\\gamma \\int_{\\mathbb R}\\operatorname{tr}(W(x)^{\\gamma+1/2})\\,dx, \\) where …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(m\\geq1\\), \\(1/2&lt;\\gamma&lt;3/2\\), and \\(p=\\gamma+1/2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(W:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb C^{m\\times m}\\) be measurable, Hermitian, and positive semidefinite, with\n  \\[\n  \\int_{\\mathbb R}\\operatorname{tr}(W(x)^p)\\,dx&lt;\\infty.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Matrix powers are defined by spectral functional calculus, and \\(\\operatorname{tr}\\) is the ordinary, unnormalized matrix trace.</p><p class=\"li\">On \\(H^1(\\mathbb R;\\mathbb C^m)\\), define\n  \\[\n  h_W[\\psi]\n  =\n  \\int_{\\mathbb R}\\|\\psi'(x)\\|^2\\,dx\n  -\n  \\int_{\\mathbb R}\\langle\\psi(x),W(x)\\psi(x)\\rangle\\,dx.\n  \\]\n  This form is closed and bounded below. Denote its associated self-adjoint operator by\n  \\[\n  H_W=-\\frac{d^2}{dx^2}-W.\n  \\]\n  Its negative spectrum consists of eigenvalues of finite multiplicity, with possible accumulation only at zero. Initially allowing the sum to be infinite, write\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{Tr}(H_W)^\\gamma_-\n  =\n  \\sum_{\\lambda_j(H_W)&lt;0}|\\lambda_j(H_W)|^\\gamma.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\operatorname{Tr}(H_W)^\\gamma_-\n\\leq\nC_\\gamma\n\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\operatorname{tr}(W(x)^{\\gamma+1/2})\\,dx,\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nC_\\gamma\n=\n\\left(\\frac{\\gamma-1/2}{\\gamma+1/2}\\right)^{\\gamma-1/2}\n\\frac{\\Gamma(\\gamma+1)}\n{\\sqrt{\\pi}\\,\\Gamma(\\gamma+3/2)}.\n\\]\nThe constant is optimal in every finite matrix dimension. With\n\\[\nr=(\\gamma-1/2)^{-1},\n\\]\nequality is attained by\n\\[\nW(x)\n=\n\\operatorname{diag}\\bigl((r+1)\\operatorname{sech}^2(rx),0,\\ldots,0\\bigr).\n\\]\nThe matrices \\(W(x)\\) may have arbitrary rank and need not commute at different points.</p>", "proof": "<p>Put\n\\[\n\\sigma=\\gamma-\\frac12,\\qquad\np=1+\\sigma,\\qquad\nq=1+\\sigma^{-1}.\n\\]\nProposition 7 establishes the form-domain and negative-spectrum assertions under the stated hypothesis on \\(W\\).</p><p>First suppose that \\(W\\) is real symmetric. If there are no negative eigenvalues, the inequality is immediate. Otherwise fix any finite nonempty list of negative eigenvalues, respecting their multiplicities. Since complex conjugation preserves each eigenspace, choose corresponding orthonormal eigenfunctions\n\\[\n\\phi_1,\\ldots,\\phi_n\n\\]\nthat are real-valued.</p><p>Choose real smooth compactly supported approximations to the \\(\\phi_i\\) in \\(H^1\\). Their Gram matrices converge to \\(I_n\\), and their form matrices converge to the diagonal matrix of the selected eigenvalues, by the potential-form bound in Proposition 7. Multiplying each approximating family by the inverse square root of its Gram matrix makes its rows orthonormal without changing their common compact support. The form matrices still converge to the same diagonal matrix. For sufficiently good approximations they are negative definite; diagonalize them by a real orthogonal change of rows.</p><p>For one such family, let\n\\[\nU\\in H_0^1(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^{n\\times m})\n\\]\nbe the matrix whose rows are the resulting functions \\(u_i\\), where the bounded interval \\(\\Omega\\) contains their supports in its interior. Extend the rows by zero when evaluating the whole-line form. They satisfy\n\\[\n\\int_\\Omega UU^T\\,dx=I_n,\n\\qquad\nh_W[u_i,u_j]=-k_i^2\\delta_{ij},\n\\qquad\nk_1\\geq\\cdots\\geq k_n&gt;0,\n\\]\nwhere \\(h_W[\\cdot,\\cdot]\\) is the polarized form, and the numbers \\(-k_i^2\\) converge, along the approximations, to the selected eigenvalues in increasing order. Set\n\\[\nK=\\operatorname{diag}(k_1,\\ldots,k_n).\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(C\\) be any real lower triangular matrix and write \\(A=CU\\). Its \\(i\\)-th row is\n\\[\na_i=\\sum_{j\\leq i}C_{ij}u_j,\n\\]\nso\n\\[\nh_W[a_i]+k_i^2\\|a_i\\|_{L^2}^2\n=\n\\sum_{j\\leq i}C_{ij}^2(k_i^2-k_j^2)\\leq0.\n\\]\nSumming over \\(i\\) bounds the quadratic terms of the action. Since \\(AA^T\\) and \\(A^TA\\) have the same nonzero eigenvalues,\n\\[\n\\mathcal E(CU)\n\\leq\n\\int_\\Omega\n\\left\\{\n\\operatorname{tr}(WA^TA)\n-\n\\operatorname{tr}((A^TA)^q)\n\\right\\}\\,dx.\n\\]</p><p>The right side has a pointwise bound that does not require its two matrices to commute. Scalar maximization gives, for \\(y,z\\geq0\\),\n\\[\nyz-z^q\\leq D_\\sigma y^p,\n\\qquad\nD_\\sigma=\\frac{\\sigma^\\sigma}{(1+\\sigma)^{1+\\sigma}}.\n\\]\nTo apply it, diagonalize an arbitrary positive semidefinite matrix \\(Z\\) in an orthonormal basis \\((v_j)\\), with eigenvalues \\(z_j\\). Set\n\\[\ny_j=\\langle v_j,Wv_j\\rangle.\n\\]\nConvexity of \\(t\\mapsto t^p\\), applied to the spectral measure of \\(W\\) in \\(v_j\\), gives\n\\[\ny_j^p\\leq\\langle v_j,W^pv_j\\rangle.\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{tr}(WZ)-\\operatorname{tr}(Z^q)\n&amp;=\n\\sum_j(y_jz_j-z_j^q)\\\\\n&amp;\\leq\nD_\\sigma\\sum_jy_j^p\n\\leq D_\\sigma\\operatorname{tr}(W^p).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nUsing \\(Z=A^TA\\), we obtain\n\\[\n\\sup_{C\\text{ lower triangular}}\\mathcal E(CU)\n\\leq\nD_\\sigma\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\operatorname{tr}(W^p)\\,dx.\n\\]\nAll terms are finite for each \\(C\\): the rows of \\(A\\) are bounded and supported on \\(\\overline\\Omega\\), and \\(W\\) is locally integrable.</p><p>Proposition 6 supplies the reverse bound in terms of the \\(k_i\\). Writing \\(\\mathrm B\\) for Euler’s beta function, the substitution \\(s=k_it\\) gives\n\\[\n\\int_{-k_i}^{k_i}(k_i^2-s^2)^\\sigma\\,ds\n=\nk_i^{2\\sigma+1}\\mathrm B\\!\\left(\\frac12,1+\\sigma\\right)\n=\nk_i^{2\\gamma}\\mathrm B\\!\\left(\\frac12,1+\\sigma\\right).\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^n k_i^{2\\gamma}\n\\leq\n\\frac{D_\\sigma}{\\mathrm B(1/2,1+\\sigma)}\n\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\operatorname{tr}(W^p)\\,dx.\n\\]\nThe coefficient is\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{D_\\sigma}{\\mathrm B(1/2,1+\\sigma)}\n&amp;=\n\\frac{\\sigma^\\sigma}{(1+\\sigma)^{1+\\sigma}}\n\\frac{\\Gamma(\\sigma+3/2)}\n{\\sqrt\\pi\\,\\Gamma(\\sigma+1)}\\\\\n&amp;=\n\\left(\\frac{\\sigma}{1+\\sigma}\\right)^\\sigma\n\\frac{\\Gamma(\\sigma+3/2)}\n{\\sqrt\\pi\\,\\Gamma(\\sigma+2)}\n=\nC_\\gamma.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</p><p>Letting the approximations converge proves the bound for the chosen finite list of eigenvalues. The sum of all negative eigenvalue moments is the supremum of these finite sums. The same bound therefore holds for \\(\\operatorname{Tr}(H_W)^\\gamma_-\\), and in particular this trace is finite.</p><p>For a complex Hermitian potential, write\n\\[\nW=E+iF\n\\]\nwith real matrices \\(E,F\\). Hermiticity means \\(E^T=E\\) and \\(F^T=-F\\), so\n\\[\n\\widetilde W=\n\\begin{pmatrix}\nE&amp;-F\\\\\nF&amp;E\n\\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nis real symmetric. The constant complex unitary\n\\[\n\\mathcal U\n=\n\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\n\\begin{pmatrix}\nI&amp;iI\\\\\nI&amp;-iI\n\\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nsatisfies\n\\[\n\\mathcal U\\widetilde W\\mathcal U^*\n=\nW\\oplus\\overline W.\n\\]\nIt follows that \\(\\widetilde W\\geq0\\) and\n\\[\n\\operatorname{tr}(\\widetilde W^p)\n=\n2\\operatorname{tr}(W^p).\n\\]\nSince \\(\\mathcal U\\) also commutes with differentiation,\n\\[\nH_{\\widetilde W}\\simeq H_W\\oplus H_{\\overline W}.\n\\]\nComplex conjugation intertwines the two summands and preserves eigenvalue multiplicities. The spectral moment for \\(\\widetilde W\\) is consequently twice that for \\(W\\). Applying the real inequality in dimension \\(2m\\) and dividing both sides by two proves the inequality for all Hermitian \\(W\\).</p><p>It remains to verify sharpness. Put\n\\[\nr=\\frac1\\sigma&gt;1,\n\\qquad\nV(x)=(r+1)\\operatorname{sech}^2(rx),\n\\qquad\n\\psi(x)=\\operatorname{sech}^{1/r}(rx).\n\\]\nThe identity\n\\[\n\\frac{\\psi'}{\\psi}=-\\tanh(rx)\n\\]\ngives \\(H_V\\psi=-\\psi\\), and \\(\\psi\\in L^2(\\mathbb R)\\).</p><p>Define\n\\[\nQ=\\partial_x+\\tanh(rx)\n\\]\non \\(H^1(\\mathbb R)\\). Direct multiplication yields\n\\[\nH_V=Q^*Q-1,\n\\qquad\nQQ^*-1\n=\n-\\partial_x^2+(r-1)\\operatorname{sech}^2(rx)\\geq0.\n\\]\nThus \\(H_V\\geq-1\\), and its eigenspace at \\(-1\\) is \\(\\ker Q\\), which is spanned by \\(\\psi\\).</p><p>If \\(\\lambda\\in(-1,0)\\) were an eigenvalue with eigenfunction \\(u\\), then \\(Qu\\neq0\\). The bounded potential gives \\(u\\in H^2\\), so \\(Qu\\in L^2\\). The intertwining identity\n\\[\n(QQ^*-1)Qu=\\lambda Qu\n\\]\nholds in distributions. Boundedness of the partner potential then implies \\(Qu\\in H^2\\), so this identity contradicts nonnegativity. Therefore \\(-1\\) is the only negative eigenvalue and is simple.</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\n\\int_{\\mathbb R}V^p\\,dx\n=\n\\frac{(r+1)^p}{r}\\,\n\\mathrm B\\!\\left(\\frac12,p\\right)\n=\n\\frac{\\mathrm B(1/2,1+\\sigma)}{D_\\sigma}\n=\nC_\\gamma^{-1}.\n\\]\nThe scalar inequality is an equality for \\(V\\). Embedding \\(V\\) in one diagonal channel and setting the other channels to zero gives equality in every matrix dimension, proving optimality.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Integrated trace comparison", "depth": 0, "snippet": "These integrals converge absolutely in matrix norm.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(0&lt;\\sigma&lt;1\\), and put\n  \\[\n  p=1+\\sigma,\\qquad q=1+\\sigma^{-1},\\qquad\n  \\beta=\\sigma-\\frac12.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define \\(c_\\sigma&gt;0\\) by\n  \\[\n  c_\\sigma^{-1}\n  =\n  \\int_0^\\infty\n  t^\\beta\\bigl(t^{-1/2}-(t+1)^{-1/2}\\bigr)\\,dt.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(n\\geq1\\), and let \\(B,V\\in\\mathbb C^{n\\times n}\\) be Hermitian. Suppose that, for some \\(b&gt;0\\),\n  \\[\n  Y_s=(sI-B)^2,\\qquad\n  X_s=Y_s+V\\geq bI\n  \\quad\\text{for every }s\\in\\mathbb R.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(t&gt;0\\), define\n  \\[\n  R(t)=\\frac1\\pi\\int_{\\mathbb R}(X_s+tI)^{-1}\\,ds,\n  \\qquad\n  P(t)=t^{-1/2}I-R(t),\n  \\]\n  and\n  \\[\n  N=c_\\sigma\\int_0^\\infty t^\\beta P(t)\\,dt.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>These integrals converge absolutely in matrix norm. If \\(N\\geq0\\), then\n\\[\n\\operatorname{tr}(NV)\\geq\\operatorname{tr}(N^q).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The convergence assertions follow from Proposition 1.</p><p>For a scalar \\(V=v&gt;0\\), shifting the variable \\(s\\) gives\n\\[\nR(t)=(t+v)^{-1/2},\n\\qquad N=v^\\sigma,\n\\]\nso the two sides of the asserted inequality agree.</p><p>Now assume \\(N\\geq0\\). Apply Proposition 2 with\n\\[\nD=N^{1/\\sigma}.\n\\]\nSince \\(p=1+\\sigma\\) and \\(q=p/\\sigma\\),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{tr}(ND)-\\frac1p\\operatorname{tr}(D^p)\n=\n\\frac1q\\operatorname{tr}(N^q).\n\\]\nCombining the integrated square identity of Proposition 2 with the weighted trace identity of Proposition 1 gives the exact deficit formula\n\\[\n\\operatorname{tr}(NV)-\\operatorname{tr}(N^q)\n=\nc_\\sigma\\int_0^\\infty\nt^\\beta\n\\left(\n\\operatorname{tr}(R(t)Z(t)^2)\n+\nq\\,\\mathcal D_{N^{1/\\sigma}}(t)\n\\right)\\,dt.\n\\]\nBoth terms in the integrand are nonnegative. Therefore\n\\[\n\\operatorname{tr}(NV)\\geq\\operatorname{tr}(N^q).\n\\]</p><p>As recorded in paper Remark 3.3, if \\(B,V\\) commute, the hypothesis implies \\(V\\geq bI\\), and simultaneous diagonalization gives\n\\[\nR(t)=(tI+V)^{-1/2},\n\\qquad N=V^\\sigma.\n\\]\nThen \\(Z=0\\) and \\(\\mathcal D_{N^{1/\\sigma}}=0\\), so the deficit vanishes. In general the two nonnegative terms retain the comparison despite the absence of a common eigenbasis.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The defining integrals converge absolutely in matrix norm.", "statement": "<p>(Resolvent, half-line, and weighted trace identities underlying paper Theorem 3.1.)</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(0&lt;\\sigma&lt;1\\), and set\n  \\[\n  \\beta=\\sigma-\\frac12,\\qquad q=1+\\sigma^{-1},\n  \\]\n  with\n  \\[\n  c_\\sigma^{-1}\n  =\n  \\int_0^\\infty\n  t^\\beta\\bigl(t^{-1/2}-(t+1)^{-1/2}\\bigr)\\,dt.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(B,V\\in\\mathbb C^{n\\times n}\\) be Hermitian, and suppose\n  \\[\n  Y_s=(sI-B)^2,\\qquad X_s=Y_s+V\\geq bI\n  \\quad(s\\in\\mathbb R)\n  \\]\n  for some \\(b&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  R(t)=\\frac1\\pi\\int_{\\mathbb R}(X_s+tI)^{-1}\\,ds,\n  \\quad\n  P(t)=t^{-1/2}I-R(t),\n  \\quad\n  N=c_\\sigma\\int_0^\\infty t^\\beta P(t)\\,dt.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The defining integrals converge absolutely in matrix norm. For each \\(t&gt;0\\), there are Hermitian matrices \\(S(t)\\) and \\(T(t)\\) such that, with\n\\[\nL(t)=\\frac{S(t)+T(t)}2=R(t)^{-1}&gt;0,\n\\qquad\nZ(t)=\\frac{S(t)-T(t)}2,\n\\]\none has\n\\[\nV+tI=L^2+Z^2+i[B,Z],\n\\qquad\nLZ+ZL+i[B,L]=0,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\operatorname{tr}(R(t)(V+tI))\n=\n\\operatorname{tr}L(t)-\\operatorname{tr}(R(t)Z(t)^2).\n\\]</p><p>Define\n\\[\nF(t)\n=\n\\frac{\\operatorname{tr}V}{\\sqrt t}\n-\n\\frac1\\pi\\int_{\\mathbb R}\n\\log\\frac{\\det(X_s+tI)}{\\det(Y_s+tI)}\\,ds\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nG(t)=\\operatorname{tr}\\bigl(L(t)-2\\sqrt t\\,I+tR(t)\\bigr).\n\\]\nThen \\(F\\in C^1((0,\\infty))\\), \\(F(t)\\geq0\\), \\(G(t)\\geq0\\), and\n\\[\nF(t)\n=\n\\frac{\\operatorname{tr}V}{\\sqrt t}\n-\n2\\operatorname{tr}(L(t)-\\sqrt t\\,I).\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{tr}(P(t)V)\n=\nF(t)+G(t)+\\operatorname{tr}(R(t)Z(t)^2),\n\\]\nall three terms on the right are integrable against \\(t^\\beta\\,dt\\), and\n\\[\n\\int_0^\\infty t^\\beta G(t)\\,dt\n=\n\\sigma\\int_0^\\infty t^\\beta F(t)\\,dt.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{tr}(NV)\n=\nqc_\\sigma\\int_0^\\infty t^\\beta G(t)\\,dt\n+\nc_\\sigma\\int_0^\\infty\nt^\\beta\\operatorname{tr}(R(t)Z(t)^2)\\,dt.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 1.1 proves the normalization and convergence assertions. Lemma 1.2 constructs the half-line matrices and identifies\n\\[\nL=\\frac{S+T}{2}=R(t)^{-1}.\n\\]\nLemma 1.3 supplies the algebraic identities, the logarithmic formula, and the weighted identity.</p><p>Integrating\n\\[\n\\operatorname{tr}(P(t)V)\n=\nF(t)+G(t)+\\operatorname{tr}(R(t)Z(t)^2)\n\\]\nagainst \\(c_\\sigma t^\\beta\\,dt\\), and using\n\\[\n\\int_0^\\infty t^\\beta F(t)\\,dt\n=\n\\sigma^{-1}\\int_0^\\infty t^\\beta G(t)\\,dt,\n\\]\ngives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{tr}(NV)\n=\n(1+\\sigma^{-1})c_\\sigma\\int_0^\\infty t^\\beta G(t)\\,dt\n+\nc_\\sigma\\int_0^\\infty\nt^\\beta\\operatorname{tr}(R(t)Z(t)^2)\\,dt.\n\\]\nSince \\(q=1+\\sigma^{-1}\\), this is the asserted final identity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "(Convergence and normalization.) In the setting of Proposition 1, the normalization integral is finite and positive, and …", "statement": "<p>(Convergence and normalization.)</p><p>In the setting of Proposition 1, the normalization integral is finite and positive, and\n\\[\nc_\\sigma\\int_0^\\infty\nt^\\beta\\bigl(t^{-1/2}-(t+y)^{-1/2}\\bigr)\\,dt\n=\ny^\\sigma\n\\qquad(y\\geq0).\n\\]\nFor every \\(t&gt;0\\), \\(R(t)&gt;0\\), and\n\\[\nR(t)=O(1)\\quad(t\\downarrow0).\n\\]\nFurthermore,\n\\[\n\\|P(t)\\|=\n\\begin{cases}\nO(t^{-1/2}),&amp;t\\downarrow0,\\\\\nO(t^{-3/2}),&amp;t\\to\\infty.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nIn particular, the integrals defining \\(R(t)\\) and \\(N\\) converge absolutely in matrix norm.</p>", "proof": "<p>The integrand defining \\(c_\\sigma^{-1}\\) is positive, is\n\\(O(t^{\\beta-1/2})\\) at zero, and is \\(O(t^{\\beta-3/2})\\) at infinity. Since\n\\[\n-\\frac12&lt;\\beta&lt;\\frac12,\n\\]\nthe integral is finite. Scaling gives\n\\[\nc_\\sigma\\int_0^\\infty\nt^\\beta\\bigl(t^{-1/2}-(t+y)^{-1/2}\\bigr)\\,dt\n=\ny^\\sigma.\n\\]</p><p>The matrices \\(X_s\\) and \\(Y_s\\) grow quadratically as \\(|s|\\to\\infty\\). Combined with \\(X_s\\geq bI\\), this shows that \\(X_s^{-1}\\) has an integrable matrix-norm bound on \\(\\mathbb R\\). Consequently \\(R(t)&gt;0\\) and \\(R(t)=O(1)\\) as \\(t\\downarrow0\\).</p><p>Diagonalizing \\(B\\) gives\n\\[\n\\frac1\\pi\\int_{\\mathbb R}(Y_s+tI)^{-1}\\,ds=t^{-1/2}I.\n\\]\nFor \\(t\\geq1\\), both \\(X_s+tI\\) and \\(Y_s+tI\\) are bounded below by\n\\(c(s^2+t)I\\), for some \\(c&gt;0\\) independent of \\(s,t\\). The resolvent identity, with the fixed matrix difference \\(V\\), bounds the norm of the difference of the two resolvents by\n\\[\nC(s^2+t)^{-2}.\n\\]\nIts \\(s\\)-integral is \\(O(t^{-3/2})\\). This proves the estimate at infinity; the estimate at zero follows from \\(R(t)=O(1)\\).</p><p>The range of \\(\\beta\\) now proves the absolute convergence of\n\\[\nc_\\sigma\\int_0^\\infty t^\\beta P(t)\\,dt.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "Lemma 3.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In the setting of Proposition 1, fix \\(t>0\\), write \\( \\nabla=\\partial_x-iB, \\) and consider \\( (-\\nabla^2+V+tI)\\psi=0. \\) For every \\(v\\in\\mathbb C^n\\), this equation has a unique \\(H^1\\) solution on each half-line …", "statement": "<p>In the setting of Proposition 1, fix \\(t&gt;0\\), write\n\\[\n\\nabla=\\partial_x-iB,\n\\]\nand consider\n\\[\n(-\\nabla^2+V+tI)\\psi=0.\n\\]\nFor every \\(v\\in\\mathbb C^n\\), this equation has a unique \\(H^1\\) solution on each half-line with boundary value \\(v\\) at zero.</p><p>There are Hermitian matrices \\(S=S(t)\\) and \\(T=T(t)\\) such that these solutions satisfy\n\\[\n\\nabla\\psi(0+)=-Sv,\n\\qquad\n\\nabla\\psi(0-)=Tv,\n\\]\nrespectively. They obey\n\\[\nV+tI=S^2+i[B,S]=T^2-i[B,T],\n\\qquad\n\\frac{S+T}{2}=R(t)^{-1}.\n\\]\nEvery eigenvalue of \\(B+iS\\) has strictly positive imaginary part.</p>", "proof": "<p>With inner products linear in the second argument, let\n\\[\na_t(\\phi,\\psi)\n=\n\\int\n\\bigl(\n\\langle\\nabla\\phi,\\nabla\\psi\\rangle\n+\n\\langle\\phi,(V+tI)\\psi\\rangle\n\\bigr)\\,dx.\n\\]\nOn the full line its Fourier symbol is \\(X_s+tI\\), and the lower bound and quadratic growth give\n\\[\nX_s+tI\\geq c_t(1+s^2)I\n\\]\nfor some \\(c_t&gt;0\\). Thus \\(a_t\\) is coercive on \\(H^1(\\mathbb R;\\mathbb C^n)\\). Zero extension proves the same assertion on the subspace of \\(H^1\\) functions on either half-line whose boundary trace is zero.</p><p>Choose any \\(H^1\\) extension \\(\\eta\\) of the prescribed boundary value. On the trace-zero space the form \\(a_t\\) is an equivalent Hilbert space inner product. The Riesz representation theorem gives a unique correction \\(u\\) in that space with\n\\[\na_t(\\phi,u)=-a_t(\\phi,\\eta)\n\\]\nfor every trace-zero \\(\\phi\\). Then \\(\\psi=\\eta+u\\) solves the equation weakly. Conversely, an \\(H^1\\) solution satisfies this weak identity by density of interior test functions in the trace-zero space, so coercivity proves uniqueness.</p><p>The equation implies \\(\\psi''\\in L^2\\). In particular the solutions belong to \\(H^2\\), their first derivatives have boundary traces, and both \\(\\psi\\) and \\(\\psi'\\) tend to zero at the infinite end of the half-line. Define \\(S,T\\) by the boundary derivative relations. For the right solutions with data \\(v,w\\), integration by parts gives\n\\[\na_t(\\psi_v,\\psi_w)=\\langle v,Sw\\rangle;\n\\]\non the left it gives\n\\[\na_t(\\psi_v,\\psi_w)=\\langle v,Tw\\rangle.\n\\]\nSince \\(a_t\\) is Hermitian, both boundary matrices are Hermitian.</p><p>Translation and uniqueness imply\n\\[\n\\nabla\\psi=-S\\psi\\quad\\text{on }(0,\\infty),\n\\qquad\n\\nabla\\psi=T\\psi\\quad\\text{on }(-\\infty,0).\n\\]\nFor example,\n\\[\n\\nabla(-S\\psi)=-S\\nabla\\psi+i[B,S]\\psi.\n\\]\nSubstitution into the equation, followed by evaluation at zero for arbitrary boundary data, proves\n\\[\nV+tI=S^2+i[B,S]=T^2-i[B,T].\n\\]</p><p>Glue the right and left solutions with the same value \\(v\\) at zero. The resulting function is continuous, and its derivative has jump \\(-(S+T)v\\). On the full line it therefore satisfies\n\\[\n(-\\nabla^2+V+tI)\\psi=\\delta_0(S+T)v.\n\\]\nUsing the Fourier transform with forward kernel \\(e^{-isx}\\) and inverting at zero gives\n\\[\nv\n=\n\\frac1{2\\pi}\\int_{\\mathbb R}(X_s+tI)^{-1}\\,ds\\,(S+T)v\n=\n\\frac12R(t)(S+T)v.\n\\]\nThis inversion may first be taken in tempered distributions; the transformed function is integrable because the inverse symbol is \\(O(s^{-2})\\), so evaluation at zero is legitimate. Since \\(R(t)&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{S+T}{2}=R(t)^{-1}.\n\\]</p><p>Finally the right solutions satisfy\n\\[\n\\psi'=i(B+iS)\\psi.\n\\]\nIf \\((B+iS)v=zv\\) with \\(v\\neq0\\), uniqueness for this first-order initial-value problem gives\n\\[\n\\psi(x)=e^{izx}v.\n\\]\nIt belongs to \\(L^2(0,\\infty;\\mathbb C^n)\\), hence \\(\\operatorname{Im}z&gt;0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "(Logarithmic integral and weighted identity.) In the setting of Proposition 1, let \\(S,T\\) be the matrices of Lemma 1.2 and put \\( L=\\frac{S+T}{2}=R(t)^{-1}>0, \\qquad Z=\\frac{S-T}{2}. \\) Let \\(F,G\\) be the scalar …", "statement": "<p>(Logarithmic integral and weighted identity.)</p><p>In the setting of Proposition 1, let \\(S,T\\) be the matrices of Lemma 1.2 and put\n\\[\nL=\\frac{S+T}{2}=R(t)^{-1}&gt;0,\n\\qquad\nZ=\\frac{S-T}{2}.\n\\]\nLet \\(F,G\\) be the scalar functions defined in Proposition 1.</p><p>Then\n\\[\nV+tI=L^2+Z^2+i[B,Z],\n\\qquad\nLZ+ZL+i[B,L]=0,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\operatorname{tr}(R(V+tI))\n=\n\\operatorname{tr}L-\\operatorname{tr}(RZ^2),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nF(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{tr}V}{\\sqrt t}\n-2\\operatorname{tr}(L-\\sqrt t\\,I).\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\nF(t)=\\frac1{\\sqrt t}\n\\operatorname{tr}\\bigl((L-\\sqrt t\\,I)^2+Z^2\\bigr)\\geq0,\n\\qquad\nG(t)\\geq0,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\operatorname{tr}(P(t)V)\n=\nF(t)+G(t)+\\operatorname{tr}(R(t)Z(t)^2).\n\\]\nThe terms on the right are integrable against \\(t^\\beta\\,dt\\), and\n\\[\n\\int_0^\\infty t^\\beta G(t)\\,dt\n=\n\\sigma\\int_0^\\infty t^\\beta F(t)\\,dt.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Adding and subtracting the Riccati identities of Lemma 1.2 gives\n\\[\nV+tI=L^2+Z^2+i[B,Z],\n\\qquad\nLZ+ZL+i[B,L]=0.\n\\]\nIn an eigenbasis of \\(L\\), the second identity has diagonal entries\n\\[\n2L_{jj}Z_{jj}=0.\n\\]\nThus \\(\\operatorname{tr}Z=0\\).</p><p>Multiplying that identity on both sides by \\(R=L^{-1}\\) gives\n\\[\ni[R,B]=-(ZR+RZ).\n\\]\nCyclicity of the trace yields\n\\[\ni\\operatorname{tr}(R[B,Z])\n=\ni\\operatorname{tr}([R,B]Z)\n=\n-2\\operatorname{tr}(RZ^2).\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{tr}(R(V+tI))\n=\n\\operatorname{tr}L-\\operatorname{tr}(RZ^2).\n\\]\nThe correction\n\\[\n\\operatorname{tr}(RZ^2)=\\operatorname{tr}(ZRZ)\n\\]\nis nonnegative.</p><p>The determinants in the definition of \\(F\\) are positive. Factoring with \\((Y_s+tI)^{1/2}\\) shows that the logarithmic ratio is \\(O(|s|^{-2})\\) at infinity, so its integral converges absolutely. Its \\(t\\)-derivative is the trace of the difference of the two resolvents. This has an integrable bound locally uniform for \\(t&gt;0\\), by the resolvent identity. In particular \\(F\\) is continuously differentiable on \\((0,\\infty)\\).</p><p>The first Riccati identity gives\n\\[\nX_s+tI=(sI-B+iS)(sI-B-iS).\n\\]\nLet \\(u_j+iv_j\\), \\(1\\leq j\\leq n\\), be the eigenvalues of \\(B+iS\\), with algebraic multiplicity. Lemma 1.2 gives \\(v_j&gt;0\\). Since\n\\[\nB-iS=(B+iS)^*,\n\\]\ntaking determinants gives\n\\[\n\\det(X_s+tI)\n=\n\\prod_{j=1}^n\\bigl((s-u_j)^2+v_j^2\\bigr).\n\\]\nThe denominator in the logarithmic ratio has the same form, with centers the eigenvalues of \\(B\\) and widths all equal to \\(\\sqrt t\\).</p><p>These centers can be removed when integrating with symmetric cutoffs. Indeed, for\n\\[\nh(s)=\\log(s^2+v^2),\n\\]\nthe function \\(h\\) is even and \\(h'(s)\\to0\\) at both ends. For a fixed translation \\(a\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{d}{da}\\int_{-r}^r h(s-a)\\,ds\n=\nh(r+a)-h(r-a)\\longrightarrow0\n\\quad(r\\to\\infty),\n\\]\nuniformly for \\(a\\) in bounded intervals. Integrating in \\(a\\) shows that the translation changes the symmetric-cutoff integral by a quantity tending to zero.</p><p>After centering the factors, the scalar identity\n\\[\n\\int_{\\mathbb R}\n\\log\\frac{s^2+v^2}{s^2+w^2}\\,ds\n=\n2\\pi(v-w),\n\\qquad v,w&gt;0,\n\\]\nfollows by differentiation and integration in \\(v\\). Since\n\\[\n\\sum_jv_j=\\operatorname{tr}S=\\operatorname{tr}L,\n\\]\nwe obtain\n\\[\nF(t)\n=\n\\frac{\\operatorname{tr}V}{\\sqrt t}\n-\n2\\operatorname{tr}(L-\\sqrt t\\,I).\n\\]</p><p>For each eigenvalue \\(r&gt;0\\) of \\(R(t)\\),\n\\[\nr^{-1}-2\\sqrt t+tr\n=\n\\frac{(1-\\sqrt t\\,r)^2}{r}\\geq0,\n\\]\nso \\(G(t)\\geq0\\). Taking traces in\n\\[\nV+tI=L^2+Z^2+i[B,Z]\n\\]\nand using the formula for \\(F\\) gives\n\\[\nF(t)\n=\n\\frac1{\\sqrt t}\n\\operatorname{tr}\\bigl((L-\\sqrt t\\,I)^2+Z^2\\bigr)\\geq0.\n\\]</p><p>The formulas for \\(\\operatorname{tr}(R(V+tI))\\) and \\(F\\) imply the exact identity\n\\[\n\\operatorname{tr}(P(t)V)\n=\nF(t)+G(t)+\\operatorname{tr}(R(t)Z(t)^2).\n\\]\nIn fact\n\\[\n0\\leq F(t),G(t)\\leq\\operatorname{tr}(P(t)V),\n\\]\neven though neither \\(V\\) nor \\(P(t)\\) is assumed positive. By Lemma 1.1, both \\(F\\) and \\(G\\) are \\(O(t^{-1/2})\\) at zero and \\(O(t^{-3/2})\\) at infinity. They are therefore integrable against \\(t^\\beta\\,dt\\), and\n\\[\n\\lim_{t\\downarrow0}t^{\\beta+1}F(t)\n=\n\\lim_{t\\to\\infty}t^{\\beta+1}F(t)=0.\n\\]\nThe same exact identity proves weighted integrability of \\(\\operatorname{tr}(RZ^2)\\).</p><p>Differentiating the logarithmic definition of \\(F\\) gives\n\\[\nF'(t)\n=\n-\\frac{\\operatorname{tr}V}{2t^{3/2}}\n-\n\\operatorname{tr}(R(t)-t^{-1/2}I).\n\\]\nSubstitution from the formulas for \\(F\\) and \\(G\\) yields\n\\[\ntF'(t)=-\\frac12F(t)-G(t).\n\\]\nIntegrate the derivative of \\(t^{\\beta+1}F(t)\\) on a compact subinterval of \\((0,\\infty)\\), then use the endpoint limits. Since \\(\\beta+1/2=\\sigma\\),\n\\[\n\\int_0^\\infty t^\\beta G(t)\\,dt\n=\n\\sigma\\int_0^\\infty t^\\beta F(t)\\,dt.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( c_\\sigma\\int_0^\\infty t^\\beta F_0(t,D)\\,dt = \\frac1p\\operatorname{tr}(D^p), \\) and \\( \\mathcal D_D(t) = G(t)-\\operatorname{tr}(P(t)D)+F_0(t,D)\\geq0. \\) All terms in this identity are integrable against …", "statement": "<p>(The positive matrix square and its integrated identity.)</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(0&lt;\\sigma&lt;1\\), \\(p=1+\\sigma\\), \\(\\beta=\\sigma-1/2\\), and let \\(c_\\sigma\\) have the normalization in Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(B,V\\in\\mathbb C^{n\\times n}\\) be Hermitian, with\n  \\[\n  X_s=(sI-B)^2+V\\geq bI\n  \\quad(s\\in\\mathbb R)\n  \\]\n  for some \\(b&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  R(t)=\\frac1\\pi\\int_{\\mathbb R}(X_s+tI)^{-1}\\,ds,\n  \\quad\n  P(t)=t^{-1/2}I-R(t),\n  \\quad\n  N=c_\\sigma\\int_0^\\infty t^\\beta P(t)\\,dt,\n  \\]\n  and\n  \\[\n  G(t)=\\operatorname{tr}\\bigl(R(t)^{-1}-2\\sqrt t\\,I+tR(t)\\bigr).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For an arbitrary positive semidefinite matrix \\(D\\in\\mathbb C^{n\\times n}\\), define\n  \\[\n  F_0(t,D)\n  =\n  \\frac{\\operatorname{tr}D}{\\sqrt t}\n  -\n  2\\operatorname{tr}\\bigl((tI+D)^{1/2}-\\sqrt t\\,I\\bigr)\n  \\]\n  and\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal D_D(t)\n  =\n  \\left\\|\n  R(t)^{-1/2}-R(t)^{1/2}(tI+D)^{1/2}\n  \\right\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}^2.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nc_\\sigma\\int_0^\\infty t^\\beta F_0(t,D)\\,dt\n=\n\\frac1p\\operatorname{tr}(D^p),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\mathcal D_D(t)\n=\nG(t)-\\operatorname{tr}(P(t)D)+F_0(t,D)\\geq0.\n\\]\nAll terms in this identity are integrable against \\(t^\\beta\\,dt\\), and\n\\[\nc_\\sigma\\int_0^\\infty t^\\beta G(t)\\,dt\n=\n\\operatorname{tr}(ND)\n-\n\\frac1p\\operatorname{tr}(D^p)\n+\nc_\\sigma\\int_0^\\infty t^\\beta\\mathcal D_D(t)\\,dt.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For a scalar eigenvalue \\(d\\geq0\\), its contribution to \\(F_0\\) is\n\\[\n\\frac d{\\sqrt t}-2(\\sqrt{t+d}-\\sqrt t)\n=\n\\int_0^d\\bigl(t^{-1/2}-(t+y)^{-1/2}\\bigr)\\,dy.\n\\]\nThe integrand is nonnegative. Tonelli’s theorem and the scaling identity in Lemma 1.1, applied to each eigenvalue of \\(D\\), give\n\\[\nc_\\sigma\\int_0^\\infty t^\\beta F_0(t,D)\\,dt\n=\n\\frac1p\\operatorname{tr}(D^p).\n\\]</p><p>For \\(R=R(t)&gt;0\\), the Hilbert–Schmidt square satisfies\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal D_D(t)\n&amp;=\n\\left\\|R^{-1/2}-R^{1/2}(tI+D)^{1/2}\\right\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}^2\\\\\n&amp;=\n\\operatorname{tr}\\bigl(\nR^{-1}+R(tI+D)-2(tI+D)^{1/2}\n\\bigr)\\geq0.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThis is valid without a commutation assumption. Indeed the cross term is\n\\(\\operatorname{tr}(tI+D)^{1/2}\\), because the adjacent factors\n\\(R^{-1/2}R^{1/2}\\) cancel, while cyclicity puts the remaining quadratic term in the form \\(\\operatorname{tr}(R(tI+D))\\).</p><p>Using the definitions of \\(P,G,F_0\\), the same square reads\n\\[\n\\mathcal D_D(t)\n=\nG(t)-\\operatorname{tr}(P(t)D)+F_0(t,D).\n\\]\nAll three terms on the right are integrable against \\(t^\\beta\\,dt\\), by Proposition 1 and the preceding formula for the integral of \\(F_0\\). Hence\n\\[\nc_\\sigma\\int_0^\\infty t^\\beta G(t)\\,dt\n=\n\\operatorname{tr}(ND)\n-\n\\frac1p\\operatorname{tr}(D^p)\n+\nc_\\sigma\\int_0^\\infty t^\\beta\\mathcal D_D(t)\\,dt.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The limit defining \\(M_\\delta\\) exists locally uniformly, and \\( M_\\delta:\\mathrm{Sym}_n\\to\\mathrm{Sym}_n \\) is locally Lipschitz.", "statement": "<p>(The regularized matrix field; incorporating paper Lemmas 4.1 and 4.2.)</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(0&lt;\\sigma&lt;1\\), and put\n  \\[\n  p=1+\\sigma,\\qquad q=1+\\sigma^{-1},\\qquad\n  \\beta=\\sigma-\\frac12,\n  \\]\n  with\n  \\[\n  c_\\sigma^{-1}\n  =\n  \\int_0^\\infty\n  t^\\beta\\bigl(t^{-1/2}-(t+1)^{-1/2}\\bigr)\\,dt.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(\\delta&gt;0\\) and a positive diagonal matrix\n  \\[\n  K=\\operatorname{diag}(k_1,\\ldots,k_n),\\qquad\n  \\kappa=\\max_i k_i.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathrm{Sym}_n\\) be the real symmetric \\(n\\times n\\) matrices, with inner product \\(\\operatorname{tr}(BH)\\). Write\n  \\[\n  \\|A\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}^2=\\operatorname{tr}(A^*A).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(y\\geq0\\), define\n  \\[\n  f_\\delta(y)\n  =\n  \\frac{c_\\sigma}{\\pi}\n  \\int_0^\\infty\n  t^\\beta\n  \\left(\\frac1{t+\\delta}-\\frac1{t+\\delta+y}\\right)\\,dt.\n  \\]\n  For \\(y&lt;0\\), set \\(f_\\delta(y)=f_\\delta'(0)y\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(s\\in\\mathbb R\\), \\(B\\in\\mathrm{Sym}_n\\), define\n  \\[\n  Q_s(B)=s^2I-2sB+K^2\n  \\]\n  and\n  \\[\n  M_\\delta(B)\n  =\n  \\lim_{r\\to\\infty}\n  \\int_{-r}^r\n  \\bigl(f_\\delta(Q_s(B))-f_\\delta(s^2)I\\bigr)\\,ds.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  \\mu_\\delta(z)\n  =\n  \\int_{\\mathbb R}\n  \\bigl(f_\\delta(s^2+z)-f_\\delta(s^2)\\bigr)\\,ds,\n  \\qquad z\\in\\mathbb R.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The limit defining \\(M_\\delta\\) exists locally uniformly, and\n\\[\nM_\\delta:\\mathrm{Sym}_n\\to\\mathrm{Sym}_n\n\\]\nis locally Lipschitz. There is a \\(C^1\\) function \\(J_\\delta:\\mathrm{Sym}_n\\to\\mathbb R\\), normalized by \\(J_\\delta(0)=0\\), such that\n\\[\ndJ_\\delta(B)[H]\n=\n-\\operatorname{tr}(M_\\delta(B)H).\n\\]</p><p>The scalar function \\(\\mu_\\delta\\) is well-defined, locally Lipschitz, and strictly increasing, with \\(\\mu_\\delta(0)=0\\). For \\(z\\geq0\\),\n\\[\n\\mu_\\delta(z)=(z+\\delta)^\\sigma-\\delta^\\sigma.\n\\]</p><p>For every \\(B\\in\\mathrm{Sym}_n\\) and every real unit vector \\(e\\),\n\\[\ne^TM_\\delta(B)e\n\\leq\n\\mu_\\delta\\bigl(e^TK^2e-(e^TBe)^2\\bigr).\n\\]\nFor a standard basis vector \\(e_i\\) and \\(y\\in\\mathbb R\\),\n\\[\nBe_i=ye_i\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nM_\\delta(B)e_i=\\mu_\\delta(k_i^2-y^2)e_i.\n\\]\nIf \\(S\\) is diagonal with entries in \\(\\{1,-1\\}\\), then\n\\[\nM_\\delta(SBS)=SM_\\delta(B)S.\n\\]\nFinally,\n\\[\nJ_\\delta(-K)-J_\\delta(K)\n=\n\\sum_{i=1}^n\n\\int_{-k_i}^{k_i}\\mu_\\delta(k_i^2-s^2)\\,ds.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 3.1 proves existence, local Lipschitz regularity, and the primitive identity for \\(M_\\delta\\). Lemma 3.2 proves the scalar properties and the formula for \\(\\mu_\\delta\\). Lemma 3.3 gives the directional estimate, coordinate identity, sign equivariance, and endpoint difference. Together these are the assertions of the proposition.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "Lemma 4.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In the setting of Proposition 3, the limit defining \\(M_\\delta\\) exists locally uniformly on \\(\\mathrm{Sym}_n\\), and \\(M_\\delta\\) is locally Lipschitz.", "statement": "<p>In the setting of Proposition 3, the limit defining \\(M_\\delta\\) exists locally uniformly on \\(\\mathrm{Sym}_n\\), and \\(M_\\delta\\) is locally Lipschitz. There is a \\(C^1\\) function \\(J_\\delta\\), normalized by \\(J_\\delta(0)=0\\), such that\n\\[\ndJ_\\delta(B)[H]\n=\n-\\operatorname{tr}(M_\\delta(B)H)\n\\qquad(B,H\\in\\mathrm{Sym}_n).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Differentiation under the integral defining \\(f_\\delta\\), followed by the change of variable \\(t=(y+\\delta)u\\), gives\n\\[\nf_\\delta'(y)\n=\na_\\sigma(y+\\delta)^{\\beta-1},\n\\qquad\na_\\sigma\n=\n\\frac{c_\\sigma}{\\pi}\n\\int_0^\\infty\\frac{u^\\beta}{(1+u)^2}\\,du&gt;0\n\\quad(y\\geq0).\n\\]\nThe integrals converge because \\(-1/2&lt;\\beta&lt;1/2\\). Thus \\(f_\\delta\\) is \\(C^1\\), strictly increasing, and concave on \\(\\mathbb R\\).</p><p>We first recall a useful estimate for the Hilbert–Schmidt norm. If \\(h\\) is scalar Lipschitz on an interval containing the spectra of Hermitian matrices \\(X,Y\\), then\n\\[\n\\|h(X)-h(Y)\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}\n\\leq\n\\operatorname{Lip}(h)\\|X-Y\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}.\n\\]\nIndeed, in eigenbases \\((e_i)\\) of \\(X\\) and \\((v_j)\\) of \\(Y\\), the \\((i,j)\\) entries of these two differences are respectively\n\\[\n(h(\\lambda_i)-h(\\nu_j))\\langle e_i,v_j\\rangle\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n(\\lambda_i-\\nu_j)\\langle e_i,v_j\\rangle.\n\\]\nThe scalar Lipschitz bound, followed by summation of their squared absolute values, proves the estimate.</p><p>Restrict \\(B\\) to a bounded set. For sufficiently large \\(|s|\\), the spectrum of\n\\[\nE_s(B)=-2sB+K^2\n\\]\nlies in \\([-a|s|,a|s|]\\), with \\(a\\) independent of \\(B\\). On this interval put\n\\[\nh_s(y)\n=\nf_\\delta(s^2+y)-f_\\delta(s^2)-f_\\delta'(s^2)y.\n\\]\nThe derivative formula gives\n\\[\n\\sup_{|y|\\leq a|s|}|h_s'(y)|\n=\nO(|s|^{2\\beta-3}).\n\\]\nHere \\(s^2+y\\) is positive for large \\(|s|\\). Since \\(h_s(0)=0\\) and\n\\(\\|E_s(B)\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}=O(|s|)\\), the Hilbert–Schmidt estimate gives\n\\[\nf_\\delta(Q_s(B))-f_\\delta(s^2)I\n=\n-2sf_\\delta'(s^2)B+T_s(B),\n\\]\nwhere, uniformly on the chosen bounded set,\n\\[\n\\|T_s(B)\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}=O(|s|^{2\\beta-2})\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\|T_s(B_1)-T_s(B_2)\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}\n\\leq\nC|s|^{2\\beta-2}\\|B_1-B_2\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}.\n\\]\nFor the first bound, include \\(f_\\delta'(s^2)K^2\\) in \\(T_s\\); for the second, use\n\\[\nE_s(B_1)-E_s(B_2)=-2s(B_1-B_2).\n\\]</p><p>The first term integrates to zero on \\([-r,r]\\), while\n\\[\n2\\beta-2=2\\sigma-3&lt;-1\n\\]\nmakes both remainder bounds integrable at infinity. On bounded \\(s\\)-intervals, the same Hilbert–Schmidt estimate and the scalar Lipschitz bound on \\(f_\\delta\\) give local Lipschitz continuity directly. This proves convergence and regularity.</p><p>To construct the primitive, choose \\(g_\\delta\\) with \\(g_\\delta'=f_\\delta\\). The trace differentiation formula is\n\\[\nd\\bigl(\\operatorname{tr}g_\\delta(X)\\bigr)[H]\n=\n\\operatorname{tr}(f_\\delta(X)H).\n\\]\nIt follows first for polynomials by cyclicity of the trace and then for \\(g_\\delta\\) by polynomial approximation in \\(C^1\\) on compact intervals containing the relevant spectra.</p><p>For \\(s\\neq0\\), the function\n\\[\nB\\longmapsto\n\\frac{\\operatorname{tr}\\bigl(g_\\delta(Q_s(B))-g_\\delta(K^2)\\bigr)}{2s}\n+\nf_\\delta(s^2)\\operatorname{tr}B\n\\]\ntherefore has differential\n\\[\n-\\operatorname{tr}\\bigl(\n(f_\\delta(Q_s(B))-f_\\delta(s^2)I)H\n\\bigr).\n\\]\nAt \\(s=0\\) the field is constant and has a linear primitive. It follows that the integral of the negative field around every piecewise smooth closed curve is zero at each finite cutoff. Local uniform convergence passes this identity to \\(M_\\delta\\). Path integration on the vector space \\(\\mathrm{Sym}_n\\) now defines \\(J_\\delta\\), with the asserted differential; continuity of \\(M_\\delta\\) gives \\(J_\\delta\\in C^1\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "(Scalar shifts.) In the setting of Proposition 3, the integral defining \\(\\mu_\\delta(z)\\) is absolutely convergent for every \\(z\\in\\mathbb R\\).", "statement": "<p>(Scalar shifts.)</p><p>In the setting of Proposition 3, the integral defining \\(\\mu_\\delta(z)\\) is absolutely convergent for every \\(z\\in\\mathbb R\\). The function \\(\\mu_\\delta\\) is locally Lipschitz and strictly increasing, with \\(\\mu_\\delta(0)=0\\). For \\(z\\geq0\\),\n\\[\n\\mu_\\delta(z)=(z+\\delta)^\\sigma-\\delta^\\sigma.\n\\]\nFor every \\(a,z\\in\\mathbb R\\),\n\\[\n\\lim_{r\\to\\infty}\n\\int_{-r}^r\n\\bigl(\nf_\\delta((s-a)^2+z)-f_\\delta(s^2)\n\\bigr)\\,ds\n=\n\\mu_\\delta(z).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>On bounded sets of \\(z\\), the integrand defining \\(\\mu_\\delta\\) and its Lipschitz constant in \\(z\\) are \\(O(|s|^{2\\beta-2})\\) at infinity. Consequently the integral is absolutely convergent and \\(\\mu_\\delta\\) is locally Lipschitz. Strict increase of \\(f_\\delta\\) implies that \\(\\mu_\\delta\\) is strictly increasing, and \\(\\mu_\\delta(0)=0\\).</p><p>For \\(z\\geq0\\), Tonelli’s theorem and\n\\[\n\\frac1\\pi\\int_{\\mathbb R}(s^2+a)^{-1}\\,ds=a^{-1/2}\n\\]\ngive\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mu_\\delta(z)\n&amp;=\nc_\\sigma\\int_0^\\infty\nt^\\beta\n\\bigl(\n(t+\\delta)^{-1/2}-(t+\\delta+z)^{-1/2}\n\\bigr)\\,dt\\\\\n&amp;=(z+\\delta)^\\sigma-\\delta^\\sigma.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe last equality is the difference of the scaling identity in Lemma 1.1 at \\(y=\\delta+z\\) and \\(y=\\delta\\).</p><p>To justify translation when the individual terms are not integrable, fix \\(z\\in\\mathbb R\\) and set\n\\[\nF_z(s)=f_\\delta(s^2+z).\n\\]\nThis function is even, and\n\\[\nF_z'(s)=O(|s|^{2\\beta-1})\\longrightarrow0\n\\quad\\text{as }|s|\\to\\infty.\n\\]\nFor fixed \\(a\\in\\mathbb R\\) and \\(r&gt;|a|\\), evenness gives\n\\[\n\\left|\n\\int_{-r}^r(F_z(s-a)-F_z(s))\\,ds\n\\right|\n\\leq\n|a|^2\n\\sup_{r-|a|\\leq u\\leq r+|a|}|F_z'(u)|.\n\\]\nFor example, when \\(a\\geq0\\), the difference consists of the integral over\n\\([r,r+a]\\) minus the integral over \\([r-a,r]\\); pairing these intervals proves the estimate. Its right-hand side tends to zero. Thus\n\\[\n\\lim_{r\\to\\infty}\n\\int_{-r}^r\n\\bigl(\nf_\\delta((s-a)^2+z)-f_\\delta(s^2)\n\\bigr)\\,ds\n=\n\\mu_\\delta(z).\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.3", "orig": "Lemma 4.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In the setting of Proposition 3, for every \\(B\\in\\mathrm{Sym}_n\\) and every real unit vector \\(e\\), \\( e^TM_\\delta(B)e \\leq \\mu_\\delta\\bigl(e^TK^2e-(e^TBe)^2\\bigr). \\) For a standard basis vector \\(e_i\\) and …", "statement": "<p>In the setting of Proposition 3, for every \\(B\\in\\mathrm{Sym}_n\\) and every real unit vector \\(e\\),\n\\[\ne^TM_\\delta(B)e\n\\leq\n\\mu_\\delta\\bigl(e^TK^2e-(e^TBe)^2\\bigr).\n\\]\nFor a standard basis vector \\(e_i\\) and \\(y\\in\\mathbb R\\),\n\\[\nBe_i=ye_i\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nM_\\delta(B)e_i=\\mu_\\delta(k_i^2-y^2)e_i.\n\\]\nFor every diagonal sign matrix \\(S\\),\n\\[\nM_\\delta(SBS)=SM_\\delta(B)S.\n\\]\nFinally,\n\\[\nJ_\\delta(-K)-J_\\delta(K)\n=\n\\sum_{i=1}^n\n\\int_{-k_i}^{k_i}\\mu_\\delta(k_i^2-s^2)\\,ds.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Scalar concavity, applied in an orthonormal eigenbasis of \\(Q_s(B)\\), gives\n\\[\ne^Tf_\\delta(Q_s(B))e\n\\leq\nf_\\delta(e^TQ_s(B)e).\n\\]\nSet\n\\[\na=e^TBe,\\qquad z=e^TK^2e-a^2.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\ne^TQ_s(B)e=(s-a)^2+z.\n\\]\nSubtract \\(f_\\delta(s^2)\\), integrate over \\([-r,r]\\), and use Lemma 3.2 to obtain the directional estimate.</p><p>If \\(Be_i=ye_i\\), the vector \\(e_i\\) is also an eigenvector of \\(Q_s(B)\\), with eigenvalue\n\\[\n(s-y)^2+k_i^2-y^2.\n\\]\nFunctional calculus and the translation identity in Lemma 3.2 prove\n\\[\nM_\\delta(B)e_i=\\mu_\\delta(k_i^2-y^2)e_i.\n\\]</p><p>For a diagonal sign matrix \\(S\\), use \\(SK^2S=K^2\\) to obtain\n\\[\nQ_s(SBS)=SQ_s(B)S,\n\\]\nand then conjugate inside the defining integral for \\(M_\\delta\\).</p><p>On the diagonal subspace, the coordinate identity and the primitive identity of Lemma 3.1 give\n\\[\ndJ_\\delta(\\operatorname{diag}(b_1,\\ldots,b_n))\n=\n-\\sum_{i=1}^n\\mu_\\delta(k_i^2-b_i^2)\\,db_i.\n\\]\nIntegrating from \\((k_1,\\ldots,k_n)\\) to \\((-k_1,\\ldots,-k_n)\\) within this subspace proves the endpoint formula.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Trace estimate at positive values of the field", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\operatorname{tr}\\bigl(M_\\delta(B)(K^2-B^2)\\bigr) \\geq \\operatorname{tr}(M_\\delta(B)^q)-E_\\delta, \\) where \\( E_\\delta = n\\bigl(\\delta\\kappa^{2\\sigma} + \\delta^\\sigma(\\kappa^2+\\delta)\\bigr). \\) In particular …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(0&lt;\\sigma&lt;1\\), \\(q=1+\\sigma^{-1}\\), and \\(\\delta&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  K=\\operatorname{diag}(k_1,\\ldots,k_n),\\qquad k_i&gt;0,\n  \\qquad \\kappa=\\max_i k_i.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(M_\\delta\\) be the field defined in Proposition 3 for these \\(\\sigma,\\delta,K\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(B\\in\\mathrm{Sym}_n\\) satisfy \\(M_\\delta(B)\\geq0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\operatorname{tr}\\bigl(M_\\delta(B)(K^2-B^2)\\bigr)\n\\geq\n\\operatorname{tr}(M_\\delta(B)^q)-E_\\delta,\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nE_\\delta\n=\nn\\bigl(\\delta\\kappa^{2\\sigma}\n+\n\\delta^\\sigma(\\kappa^2+\\delta)\\bigr).\n\\]\nIn particular \\(E_\\delta\\to0\\) as \\(\\delta\\downarrow0\\) for fixed \\(K\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(M=M_\\delta(B)\\). By the directional estimate in Proposition 3, its positivity and the strict increase of \\(\\mu_\\delta\\) imply\n\\[\ne^TK^2e\\geq(e^TBe)^2\n\\]\nfor every real unit vector \\(e\\). Consequently\n\\[\ne^TQ_s(B)e\n=\n(s-e^TBe)^2+e^TK^2e-(e^TBe)^2\n\\geq0,\n\\]\nand hence \\(Q_s(B)\\geq0\\) for all \\(s\\in\\mathbb R\\). This is positivity also on \\(\\mathbb C^n\\): for \\(z=u+iv\\), with real \\(u,v\\), symmetry gives\n\\[\nz^*Q_s(B)z=u^TQ_s(B)u+v^TQ_s(B)v.\n\\]</p><p>The same directional estimate, together with the scalar formula for \\(\\mu_\\delta\\), gives\n\\[\n0\\leq M\\leq\\mu_\\delta(\\kappa^2)I\\leq\\kappa^{2\\sigma}I.\n\\]\nThe last inequality uses\n\\[\n(a+b)^\\sigma\\leq a^\\sigma+b^\\sigma\n\\qquad(a,b\\geq0,\\ 0&lt;\\sigma&lt;1).\n\\]</p><p>Put\n\\[\nD=K^2-B^2,\\qquad V=D+\\delta I,\\qquad\nY_s=(sI-B)^2.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nX_s=Y_s+V=Q_s(B)+\\delta I\\geq\\delta I.\n\\]\nLet \\(R(t),N\\) be the resolvent average and integrated matrix for this \\(B,V\\), as in Proposition 1. We claim that\n\\[\nN=M+\\delta^\\sigma I.\n\\]</p><p>Diagonalize \\(B\\) and apply the translation identity in Proposition 3 with \\(z=0\\) to each diagonal entry. This allows \\(f_\\delta(s^2)I\\) in the definition of \\(M_\\delta\\) to be replaced by \\(f_\\delta(Y_s)\\). Both \\(Q_s(B)\\) and \\(Y_s\\) are positive semidefinite, so the definition of \\(f_\\delta\\) expresses the resulting difference as\n\\[\n\\frac{c_\\sigma}{\\pi}\n\\int_0^\\infty\nt^\\beta\n\\bigl(\n(Y_s+(t+\\delta)I)^{-1}\n-\n(Q_s(B)+(t+\\delta)I)^{-1}\n\\bigr)\\,dt.\n\\]</p><p>For fixed \\(B,\\delta&gt;0\\), positivity and quadratic growth in \\(s\\) give a common lower bound\n\\[\nc(1+t+s^2)I\n\\]\nfor the two matrices being inverted. Their difference before inversion is the constant matrix \\(D\\). The resolvent identity therefore bounds the norm of the weighted difference by\n\\[\nC\\|D\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}t^\\beta(1+t+s^2)^{-2}.\n\\]\nThis is integrable on \\((0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R\\): integration in \\(s\\) leaves a constant times\n\\[\nt^\\beta(1+t)^{-3/2},\n\\]\nintegrable because \\(-1&lt;\\beta&lt;1/2\\). Fubini’s theorem gives\n\\[\nM\n=\nc_\\sigma\\int_0^\\infty\nt^\\beta\\bigl((t+\\delta)^{-1/2}I-R(t)\\bigr)\\,dt.\n\\]\nSubtract this identity from the definition of \\(N\\) and use the scaling identity of Proposition 1 with \\(y=\\delta\\). This proves\n\\[\nN=M+\\delta^\\sigma I.\n\\]</p><p>In particular \\(N\\geq0\\). The final identity of Proposition 1 and Proposition 2, with its auxiliary positive matrix chosen as \\(N^{1/\\sigma}\\), give\n\\[\n\\operatorname{tr}(NV)\\geq\\operatorname{tr}(N^q).\n\\]\nIndeed,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{tr}(N N^{1/\\sigma})\n-\\frac1p\\operatorname{tr}\\bigl((N^{1/\\sigma})^p\\bigr)\n=\n\\frac1q\\operatorname{tr}(N^q),\n\\]\nand both remainder integrals are nonnegative.</p><p>Therefore\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{tr}(MD)\n&amp;=\n\\operatorname{tr}(NV)-\\delta\\operatorname{tr}M\n-\\delta^\\sigma\\operatorname{tr}V\\\\\n&amp;\\geq\n\\operatorname{tr}(N^q)-\\delta\\operatorname{tr}M\n-\\delta^\\sigma\\operatorname{tr}V.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nSince \\(N=M+\\delta^\\sigma I\\), the matrices \\(N,M\\) have a common eigenbasis and\n\\[\n\\operatorname{tr}(N^q)\\geq\\operatorname{tr}(M^q).\n\\]\nThe preceding bound on \\(M\\) gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{tr}M\\leq n\\kappa^{2\\sigma},\n\\]\nwhile\n\\[\nV=K^2-B^2+\\delta I\\leq(\\kappa^2+\\delta)I\n\\]\ngives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{tr}V\\leq n(\\kappa^2+\\delta).\n\\]\nSubstitution proves the stated estimate.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Connecting paths", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every \\(\\varepsilon>0\\), there are \\(C_\\varepsilon\\in\\mathcal L\\) and \\( B_\\varepsilon\\in C^1(\\overline\\Omega;\\mathrm{Sym}_n) \\) such that, with \\(A_\\varepsilon=C_\\varepsilon U\\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(n,d\\geq1\\), let \\(\\Omega=(x_-,x_+)\\) be bounded, and let\n  \\[\n  K=\\operatorname{diag}(k_1,\\ldots,k_n),\\qquad k_i&gt;0,\n  \\qquad\\kappa=\\max_i k_i.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(0&lt;\\sigma&lt;1\\), \\(\\delta&gt;0\\), and let \\(M_\\delta\\) be the field of Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose\n  \\[\n  U\\in H_0^1(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^{n\\times d}),\n  \\qquad\n  \\int_\\Omega U(x)U(x)^T\\,dx=I_n.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(\\mathcal L\\) for the real lower triangular \\(n\\times n\\) matrices.</p><p class=\"li\">For the proof, choose a smooth nonnegative compactly supported function\n  \\(\\chi\\) on \\(\\mathrm{Sym}_n\\), equal to one on\n  \\(\\{B:\\|B\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}\\leq\\kappa\\}\\), and invariant under conjugation by diagonal sign matrices. Set\n  \\[\n  \\widehat M_\\delta(B)=\\chi(B)M_\\delta(B).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For fixed \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), \\(C\\in\\mathcal L\\), and \\(0\\leq\\theta\\leq1\\), define the endpoint map by solving\n  \\[\n  \\varepsilon B'\n  =\n  \\theta\\widehat M_\\delta(B)-(CU)(CU)^T,\n  \\qquad B(x_-)=K,\n  \\]\n  and setting\n  \\[\n  \\Phi(C,\\theta)=B(x_+)+K.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), there are \\(C_\\varepsilon\\in\\mathcal L\\) and\n\\[\nB_\\varepsilon\\in C^1(\\overline\\Omega;\\mathrm{Sym}_n)\n\\]\nsuch that, with \\(A_\\varepsilon=C_\\varepsilon U\\),\n\\[\n\\varepsilon B_\\varepsilon'\n=\nM_\\delta(B_\\varepsilon)-A_\\varepsilon A_\\varepsilon^T,\n\\qquad\nB_\\varepsilon(x_-)=K,\\qquad\nB_\\varepsilon(x_+)=-K.\n\\]\nThese choices satisfy\n\\[\n\\sup_{x\\in\\overline\\Omega}\\|B_\\varepsilon(x)\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}\\leq\\kappa,\n\\qquad\n\\sup_{0&lt;\\varepsilon\\leq1}\\|C_\\varepsilon\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}&lt;\\infty,\n\\]\nwhere the second bound is for fixed \\(\\delta,K,\\Omega\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For each \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), Lemma 5.2 gives \\(C_\\varepsilon\\in\\mathcal L\\) with\n\\[\n\\Phi(C_\\varepsilon,1)=0.\n\\]\nLet \\(B_\\varepsilon\\) be the corresponding \\(C^1\\) path and set\n\\[\nA_\\varepsilon=C_\\varepsilon U.\n\\]\nThe initial condition and vanishing endpoint map give\n\\[\nB_\\varepsilon(x_-)=K,\\qquad B_\\varepsilon(x_+)=-K.\n\\]\nLemma 5.3 confines the path to \\(\\|B_\\varepsilon\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}\\leq\\kappa\\), removes the cutoff, and proves the required path equation. The bound in Lemma 5.1 gives\n\\[\n\\sup_{0&lt;\\varepsilon\\leq1}\\|C_\\varepsilon\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}&lt;\\infty\n\\]\nfor fixed \\(\\delta,K,\\Omega\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "(Endpoint map:", "statement": "<p>(Endpoint map: compactness, row signs, and initial zeros.)</p><p>In the setting of Proposition 5, fix \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\). The endpoint map \\(\\Phi\\) is well-defined and continuous.</p><p>Let \\(\\mathcal G\\) be the group of diagonal matrices with entries in \\(\\{1,-1\\}\\). It acts on \\(\\mathcal L\\) by \\(C\\mapsto SC\\) and on \\(\\mathrm{Sym}_n\\) by \\(B\\mapsto SBS\\). Then\n\\[\n\\Phi(SC,\\theta)=S\\Phi(C,\\theta)S.\n\\]</p><p>The joint zero set\n\\[\n\\{(C,\\theta)\\in\\mathcal L\\times[0,1]:\\Phi(C,\\theta)=0\\}\n\\]\nis compact, and the \\(\\mathcal G\\)-action is free at every zero. At every zero,\n\\[\nCC^T\n=\n2\\varepsilon K\n+\n\\theta\\int_\\Omega\\widehat M_\\delta(B(x))\\,dx.\n\\]\nConsequently, for \\(0&lt;\\varepsilon\\leq1\\),\n\\[\n\\|C\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}^2\n\\leq\n2\\operatorname{tr}K\n+\nn|\\Omega|\n\\sup_{B\\in\\mathrm{Sym}_n}\n\\|\\widehat M_\\delta(B)\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}.\n\\]</p><p>At \\(\\theta=0\\),\n\\[\n\\Phi(C,0)=2K-\\varepsilon^{-1}CC^T.\n\\]\nIts zeros in \\(\\mathcal L\\) are exactly\n\\[\nC=\\operatorname{diag}(d_1,\\ldots,d_n),\n\\qquad d_i=\\pm\\sqrt{2\\varepsilon k_i}.\n\\]\nThey form one orbit under \\(\\mathcal G\\), and each is a regular zero with respect to \\(C\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the continuous representative of \\(U\\), available because the interval is bounded and \\(U\\in H^1\\). The cutoff \\(\\chi\\) can be obtained from any cutoff of a neighborhood of the indicated compact set by averaging over the finitely many sign matrices. By Proposition 3,\n\\[\n\\widehat M_\\delta(B)=\\chi(B)M_\\delta(B)\n\\]\nis bounded and globally Lipschitz.</p><p>The bounded Lipschitz field and continuous forcing give a unique \\(C^1\\) solution on the whole interval. Continuous dependence on the parameters makes \\(\\Phi\\) continuous. Equivariance of the field and \\(SKS=K\\), together with uniqueness, give\n\\[\n\\Phi(SC,\\theta)=S\\Phi(C,\\theta)S.\n\\]</p><p>At a zero, integrate the differential equation and use\n\\[\n\\int_\\Omega UU^T\\,dx=I_n\n\\]\nto obtain\n\\[\nCC^T\n=\n2\\varepsilon K\n+\n\\theta\\int_\\Omega\\widehat M_\\delta(B(x))\\,dx.\n\\]\nTaking traces gives the stated uniform bound when \\(0&lt;\\varepsilon\\leq1\\). For each fixed \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), the same calculation gives a finite bound. Thus the joint zero set is compact.</p><p>To prove freeness, it suffices to show that no row of \\(C\\) vanishes. If row \\(i\\) vanished, the reflection \\(S_i\\) changing only its sign would fix \\(C\\). Equivariance and uniqueness would then force\n\\[\nS_iB(x)S_i=B(x),\n\\]\nso \\(B(x)e_i=y(x)e_i\\) for a scalar function \\(y\\). The coordinate identity in Proposition 3 would give\n\\[\n\\varepsilon y'\n=\n\\theta\\chi(B(x))\\mu_\\delta(k_i^2-y^2),\n\\qquad y(x_-)=k_i.\n\\]\nHere \\(\\chi(B(x))\\) is a fixed continuous coefficient. Since \\(\\mu_\\delta\\) is locally Lipschitz and \\(\\mu_\\delta(0)=0\\), uniqueness for this scalar equation gives \\(y\\equiv k_i\\). This contradicts \\(y(x_+)=-k_i\\).</p><p>At \\(\\theta=0\\), direct integration gives\n\\[\n\\Phi(C,0)=2K-\\varepsilon^{-1}CC^T.\n\\]\nThe first row of \\(CC^T=2\\varepsilon K\\) determines\n\\(d_1=\\pm\\sqrt{2\\varepsilon k_1}\\) and forces the remaining entries in the first column of \\(C\\) to vanish; induction gives the stated diagonal zeros. These \\(2^n\\) zeros form one orbit.</p><p>For a lower triangular variation \\(H\\), the derivative with respect to \\(C\\) at such a zero has diagonal entries\n\\[\n-2\\varepsilon^{-1}d_iH_{ii}\n\\]\nand entries\n\\[\n-\\varepsilon^{-1}d_jH_{ij}\n\\qquad(i&gt;j).\n\\]\nIt is therefore an isomorphism between vector spaces of dimension \\(n(n+1)/2\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "(Continuation after identifying row signs.) In the setting of Proposition 5, for every fixed \\(\\varepsilon>0\\), the endpoint map has a zero at \\(\\theta=1\\):", "statement": "<p>(Continuation after identifying row signs.)</p><p>In the setting of Proposition 5, for every fixed \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), the endpoint map has a zero at \\(\\theta=1\\):\n\\[\n\\Phi(C,1)=0\n\\quad\\text{for some }C\\in\\mathcal L.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>By Lemma 5.1, the zero set is compact and lies entirely where the sign action is free. After taking the quotient by that action, there is a single regular zero at the initial parameter.</p><p>Suppose, to the contrary, that \\(\\Phi(C,1)\\) never vanishes. Compactness allows us to choose a bounded invariant open set \\(O\\subset\\mathcal L\\) containing the \\(C\\)-projection of every zero, with \\(\\overline O\\) contained in the open set where all rows are nonzero. There is then a positive lower bound for \\(\\|\\Phi\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}\\) on\n\\[\n(\\partial O\\times[0,1])\\cup(\\overline O\\times\\{1\\}).\n\\]</p><p>Reparametrize \\(\\theta\\) by a smooth map\n\\[\n\\rho:[0,1]\\to[0,1]\n\\]\nthat equals zero near \\(0\\) and has \\(\\rho(1)=1\\). The resulting map\n\\[\nF(C,\\theta)=\\Phi(C,\\rho(\\theta))\n\\]\nagrees with the smooth map\n\\[\n\\Phi(C,0)=2K-\\varepsilon^{-1}CC^T\n\\]\non an initial collar, for all \\(0\\leq\\theta\\leq a\\) with some \\(a&gt;0\\). It retains a positive gap on the displayed boundary set.</p><p>Approximate \\(F\\) uniformly on \\(\\overline O\\times[0,1]\\) by a smooth equivariant map \\(\\Psi\\) that still equals \\(\\Phi(C,0)\\) on a smaller initial collar. A direct construction is to extend \\(F\\) constantly in \\(\\theta\\) outside \\([0,1]\\), smooth by convolution in the finite-dimensional variables, and use a smooth function of \\(\\theta\\) to splice the approximation to \\(F(C,0)\\) inside its initial collar. Averaging the result in the form\n\\[\n\\frac1{|\\mathcal G|}\n\\sum_{S\\in\\mathcal G}S\\Psi_0(SC,\\theta)S\n\\]\nmakes it equivariant. Uniform approximation can be as close as needed, so the positive gap persists.</p><p>We next make zero a regular value in the interior by a small equivariant perturbation. The freeness of the action on \\(\\overline O\\) permits this step. Around any \\(C_0\\in\\overline O\\), choose a ball whose translates by distinct sign matrices are disjoint, and a smooth bump function \\(b\\) supported in that ball with \\(b(C_0)=1\\). For \\(H\\in\\mathrm{Sym}_n\\), the map\n\\[\nV_H(C)=\\sum_{S\\in\\mathcal G}b(SC)SHS\n\\]\nis smooth and equivariant, and \\(V_H(C_0)=H\\). Taking \\(H\\) in a basis of \\(\\mathrm{Sym}_n\\) gives maps whose values span the target near \\(C_0\\). A finite covering of \\(\\overline O\\) therefore supplies smooth equivariant maps\n\\[\nV_1,\\ldots,V_N\n\\]\nwhose values span \\(\\mathrm{Sym}_n\\) at every point of \\(\\overline O\\). The action on the target need not be free: the disjoint supports in the domain allow an arbitrary target value at a representative of each orbit.</p><p>Choose a smooth function\n\\[\nh:[0,1]\\to[0,\\infty)\n\\]\nthat vanishes near \\(0\\) and is positive thereafter, with its zero set contained in the collar where\n\\[\n\\Psi(C,\\theta)=\\Phi(C,0).\n\\]\nFor \\(\\alpha=(\\alpha_1,\\ldots,\\alpha_N)\\in\\mathbb R^N\\), put\n\\[\n\\Psi_\\alpha(C,\\theta)\n=\n\\Psi(C,\\theta)+h(\\theta)\\sum_{j=1}^N\\alpha_jV_j(C).\n\\]\nRestrict \\(\\alpha\\) to a sufficiently small open ball about zero so that the gap on the boundary set remains.</p><p>At an interior zero of the map of all three variables \\((C,\\theta,\\alpha)\\), its differential is onto. If \\(h(\\theta)&gt;0\\), the parameter directions span the target. If \\(h(\\theta)=0\\), the derivative in \\(C\\) is the isomorphism computed in Lemma 5.1.</p><p>The interior zero set of this parameter family is thus a smooth manifold. Apply Sard’s theorem to its projection onto the parameter ball. For a regular value \\(\\alpha\\) of this projection, zero is a regular value of\n\\[\n(C,\\theta)\\longmapsto\\Psi_\\alpha(C,\\theta).\n\\]\nTo see the implication, write the differential of the parameter family as \\((L_1,L_2)\\) in the \\((C,\\theta)\\) and \\(\\alpha\\) directions. Surjectivity of the differential of the projection means that, for every \\(w\\), there is a \\(v\\) with\n\\[\nL_1v+L_2w=0.\n\\]\nThus \\(\\operatorname{ran}L_2\\subset\\operatorname{ran}L_1\\); since \\((L_1,L_2)\\) is onto, \\(L_1\\) is onto as well. Sard’s theorem supplies such parameters arbitrarily close to zero.</p><p>For this choice of \\(\\alpha\\), the zero set of \\(\\Psi_\\alpha\\) in\n\\(O\\times[0,1]\\) is a compact smooth one-dimensional manifold with boundary. Its dimension follows from\n\\[\n\\dim\\mathcal L=\\dim\\mathrm{Sym}_n.\n\\]\nThere are no zeros on the indicated boundary set, and near \\(\\theta=0\\) the map is the initial map, independent of \\(\\theta\\), with invertible derivative in \\(C\\). The boundary therefore consists precisely of the \\(2^n\\) initial zeros.</p><p>The sign group acts freely on this manifold. Its quotient is again a compact one-dimensional manifold with boundary: around each point, choose a neighborhood disjoint from its nontrivial translates, so that the quotient has the same local interval or half-interval coordinate. All initial zeros belong to one orbit, so the quotient has exactly one boundary point.</p><p>This is impossible. Every compact one-dimensional manifold is a finite disjoint union of circles and closed intervals, and hence has an even number of boundary points. The contradiction proves that \\(\\Phi(\\,\\cdot\\,,1)\\) has a zero.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "(Confinement of a connecting path.) In the setting of Proposition 5, let \\(C\\in\\mathcal L\\) satisfy \\(\\Phi(C,1)=0\\), and let \\(B\\) be its initial-value solution.", "statement": "<p>(Confinement of a connecting path.)</p><p>In the setting of Proposition 5, let \\(C\\in\\mathcal L\\) satisfy \\(\\Phi(C,1)=0\\), and let \\(B\\) be its initial-value solution. Then\n\\[\n\\|B(x)\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}\\leq\\kappa\n\\quad(x\\in\\overline\\Omega).\n\\]\nConsequently \\(\\chi(B(x))=1\\) throughout the interval, and\n\\[\n\\varepsilon B'\n=\nM_\\delta(B)-(CU)(CU)^T.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For any fixed real unit vector \\(e\\), set\n\\[\ny(x)=e^TB(x)e.\n\\]\nBy the directional estimate in Proposition 3, whenever \\(|y(x)|&gt;\\kappa\\),\n\\[\ne^TM_\\delta(B(x))e\n\\leq\n\\mu_\\delta\\bigl(e^TK^2e-y(x)^2\\bigr)&lt;0.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\chi\\geq0\\) and \\((CU)(CU)^T\\geq0\\), the differential equation gives\n\\[\ny'(x)\\leq0\n\\]\non this set.</p><p>Both endpoint values of \\(y\\) lie in \\([-\\kappa,\\kappa]\\). A connected interval on which \\(y&gt;\\kappa\\) cannot begin at the level \\(\\kappa\\) while \\(y\\) is nonincreasing. Likewise, an interval on which \\(y&lt;-\\kappa\\) cannot end at the level \\(-\\kappa\\) while \\(y\\) is nonincreasing. The first endpoint excludes the former excursion, and the second endpoint excludes the latter. Thus\n\\[\n|e^TB(x)e|\\leq\\kappa\n\\]\nfor every unit vector \\(e\\) and every \\(x\\).</p><p>It follows that \\(\\|B(x)\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}\\leq\\kappa\\) throughout the interval. Hence \\(\\chi(B(x))=1\\), and the cutoff equation becomes the asserted equation.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Finite-interval action inequality", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\sup_{C\\in\\mathcal L}\\mathcal E(CU) \\geq \\sum_{i=1}^n\\int_{-k_i}^{k_i}(k_i^2-s^2)^\\sigma\\,ds. \\) The order of the \\(k_i\\) is immaterial for this proposition.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(0&lt;\\sigma&lt;1\\), and put\n  \\[\n  p=1+\\sigma,\\qquad q=1+\\sigma^{-1}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Omega=(x_-,x_+)\\) be bounded, let \\(n,d\\geq1\\), and suppose\n  \\[\n  U\\in H_0^1(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^{n\\times d}),\n  \\qquad\n  \\int_\\Omega U(x)U(x)^T\\,dx=I_n.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix\n  \\[\n  K=\\operatorname{diag}(k_1,\\ldots,k_n),\\qquad k_i&gt;0.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(A\\in H_0^1(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^{n\\times d})\\), define\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal E(A)\n  =\n  \\int_\\Omega\n  \\left(\n  \\|A'\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}^2\n  +\n  \\operatorname{tr}(K^2AA^T)\n  -\n  \\operatorname{tr}((AA^T)^q)\n  \\right)\\,dx.\n  \\]\n  This integral is finite because one-dimensional \\(H^1\\) functions on a bounded interval are continuous.</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(\\mathcal L\\) for the real lower triangular \\(n\\times n\\) matrices.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\sup_{C\\in\\mathcal L}\\mathcal E(CU)\n\\geq\n\\sum_{i=1}^n\\int_{-k_i}^{k_i}(k_i^2-s^2)^\\sigma\\,ds.\n\\]\nThe order of the \\(k_i\\) is immaterial for this proposition.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(\\delta&gt;0\\). For each \\(0&lt;\\varepsilon\\leq1\\), choose\n\\[\nC_\\varepsilon,\\qquad\nA_\\varepsilon=C_\\varepsilon U,\\qquad\nB_\\varepsilon\n\\]\nas in Proposition 5. Temporarily suppress the subscript \\(\\varepsilon\\).</p><p>Since \\(A\\in H_0^1\\) and \\(B\\in C^1\\), the function\n\\[\nx\\longmapsto\nJ_\\delta(B(x))\n+\n\\operatorname{tr}(A(x)^TB(x)A(x))\n\\]\nis absolutely continuous. The primitive identity in Proposition 3 and the path equation give, almost everywhere,\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\bigl(J_\\delta(B)+\\operatorname{tr}(A^TBA)\\bigr)'\n&amp;=\n2\\operatorname{tr}((A')^TBA)\n-\n\\operatorname{tr}\\bigl((M_\\delta(B)-AA^T)B'\\bigr)\\\\\n&amp;=\n2\\operatorname{tr}((A')^TBA)\n-\n\\varepsilon^{-1}\\|M_\\delta(B)-AA^T\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}^2\\\\\n&amp;\\leq\n\\|A'\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}^2\n+\n\\operatorname{tr}(B^2AA^T)\n-\n\\varepsilon^{-1}\\|M_\\delta(B)-AA^T\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe last line is the Hilbert–Schmidt Cauchy–Schwarz inequality followed by \\(2ab\\leq a^2+b^2\\).</p><p>Since \\(A\\) vanishes at both endpoints, integration and the definition of the action yield\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nJ_\\delta(-K)-J_\\delta(K)\n\\leq\\mathcal E(A)\n+\\int_\\Omega\\Bigl[\n&amp;\\operatorname{tr}((AA^T)^q)\n-\\operatorname{tr}((K^2-B^2)AA^T)\\\\\n&amp;-\\varepsilon^{-1}\n\\|M_\\delta(B)-AA^T\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}^2\n\\Bigr]\\,dx.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</p><p>The bounds in Proposition 5, together with boundedness of \\(U\\), place all pairs\n\\[\n(A_\\varepsilon(x),B_\\varepsilon(x)),\n\\qquad x\\in\\overline\\Omega,\\quad0&lt;\\varepsilon\\leq1,\n\\]\nin one compact set\n\\[\n\\mathcal K_\\delta\\subset\n\\mathbb R^{n\\times d}\\times\\mathrm{Sym}_n.\n\\]\nOn this set define the continuous functions\n\\[\nF(A,B)\n=\n\\operatorname{tr}((AA^T)^q)\n-\n\\operatorname{tr}((K^2-B^2)AA^T)\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nr_\\delta(A,B)\n=\n\\|M_\\delta(B)-AA^T\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}^2.\n\\]\nIf \\(r_\\delta(A,B)=0\\), then\n\\[\nM_\\delta(B)=AA^T\\geq0,\n\\]\nand Proposition 4 gives\n\\[\nF(A,B)\\leq E_\\delta.\n\\]\nThis is the only point at which positivity of the field is needed; the constructed paths themselves need not have this property.</p><p>Compactness implies\n\\[\n\\limsup_{\\varepsilon\\downarrow0}\n\\sup_{(A,B)\\in\\mathcal K_\\delta}\n\\bigl(F(A,B)-\\varepsilon^{-1}r_\\delta(A,B)\\bigr)\n\\leq E_\\delta.\n\\]\nIndeed, a failure would give \\(\\eta&gt;0\\), a sequence \\(\\varepsilon_j\\downarrow0\\), and points \\((A_j,B_j)\\in\\mathcal K_\\delta\\) at which the expression exceeds \\(E_\\delta+\\eta\\). Boundedness of \\(F\\) forces\n\\[\nr_\\delta(A_j,B_j)=O(\\varepsilon_j).\n\\]\nA convergent subsequence has limit \\((A_*,B_*)\\) with\n\\[\nr_\\delta(A_*,B_*)=0\n\\]\nand, by continuity,\n\\[\nF(A_*,B_*)\\geq E_\\delta+\\eta.\n\\]\nThis contradicts the preceding comparison.</p><p>Apply this bound to the action identity, bound\n\\(\\mathcal E(A_\\varepsilon)\\) by the supremum over lower triangular \\(C\\), and let \\(\\varepsilon\\downarrow0\\). We obtain\n\\[\nJ_\\delta(-K)-J_\\delta(K)\n\\leq\n\\sup_{C\\in\\mathcal L}\\mathcal E(CU)\n+\n|\\Omega|E_\\delta.\n\\]\nAll compactness estimates so far were for fixed \\(\\delta\\). This is sufficient: the supremum on the right is independent of \\(\\delta\\), and the remaining limit concerns only the explicit endpoint values and the error term.</p><p>By Proposition 3,\n\\[\nJ_\\delta(-K)-J_\\delta(K)\n=\n\\sum_{i=1}^n\n\\int_{-k_i}^{k_i}\\mu_\\delta(k_i^2-s^2)\\,ds.\n\\]\nFor \\(z\\geq0\\),\n\\[\n\\mu_\\delta(z)=(z+\\delta)^\\sigma-\\delta^\\sigma\n\\]\ngives\n\\[\n0\\leq\\mu_\\delta(z)\\leq z^\\sigma,\n\\qquad\n\\mu_\\delta(z)\\longrightarrow z^\\sigma\n\\quad(\\delta\\downarrow0).\n\\]\nDominated convergence sends the endpoint difference to\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^n\\int_{-k_i}^{k_i}(k_i^2-s^2)^\\sigma\\,ds.\n\\]\nSince \\(E_\\delta\\to0\\), letting \\(\\delta\\downarrow0\\) proves the action inequality.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Form domain and negative spectrum", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The form \\(h_W\\) is closed and bounded below.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(m\\geq1\\), \\(p&gt;1\\), and let \\(W:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb C^{m\\times m}\\) be measurable, Hermitian, and positive semidefinite, with\n  \\[\n  \\int_{\\mathbb R}\\operatorname{tr}(W^p)\\,dx&lt;\\infty.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  h_W[\\psi]\n  =\n  \\int_{\\mathbb R}\\|\\psi'\\|^2\\,dx\n  -\n  \\int_{\\mathbb R}\\langle\\psi,W\\psi\\rangle\\,dx,\n  \\qquad\n  \\psi\\in H^1(\\mathbb R;\\mathbb C^m).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The form \\(h_W\\) is closed and bounded below. Multiplication by \\(W^{1/2}\\) is compact from \\(H^1(\\mathbb R;\\mathbb C^m)\\) to \\(L^2(\\mathbb R;\\mathbb C^m)\\). The associated self-adjoint operator has only isolated eigenvalues of finite multiplicity below zero, with possible accumulation only at zero.</p>", "proof": "<p>Put\n\\[\nw(x)=\\|W(x)\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}.\n\\]\nPositivity gives \\(w^p\\leq\\operatorname{tr}(W^p)\\), so \\(w\\in L^p(\\mathbb R)\\). The one-dimensional Sobolev estimate\n\\[\n\\|\\psi\\|_\\infty^2\\leq2\\|\\psi\\|_2\\|\\psi'\\|_2\n\\]\nholds also for vector-valued functions, by applying the scalar estimate to their norm. Hölder’s inequality and interpolation therefore give\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\langle\\psi,W\\psi\\rangle\\,dx\n&amp;\\leq\n\\|w\\|_p\\|\\psi\\|_{2p/(p-1)}^2\\\\\n&amp;\\leq\n2^{1/p}\\|w\\|_p\n\\|\\psi'\\|_2^{1/p}\\|\\psi\\|_2^{2-1/p}\\\\\n&amp;\\leq\n\\varepsilon\\|\\psi'\\|_2^2+C_\\varepsilon\\|\\psi\\|_2^2\n\\qquad(\\varepsilon&gt;0).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe last step is Young’s inequality. Consequently a sufficiently shifted form norm is equivalent to the \\(H^1\\) norm. The potential form is continuous on \\(H^1\\), so the form is closed and bounded below.</p><p>For compactness, truncate the multiplier both in space and in size:\n\\[\nT_{R,L}\\psi\n=\n\\mathbf1_{\\{|x|\\leq R,\\ w(x)\\leq L\\}}W^{1/2}\\psi.\n\\]\nRestriction to \\([-R,R]\\), followed by the compact embedding\n\\[\nH^1([-R,R];\\mathbb C^m)\\hookrightarrow L^2([-R,R];\\mathbb C^m)\n\\]\nand bounded multiplication, shows that \\(T_{R,L}\\) is compact. If\n\\[\nT\\psi=W^{1/2}\\psi,\n\\]\nHölder’s inequality and the same Sobolev embedding imply\n\\[\n\\|(T-T_{R,L})\\psi\\|_2^2\n\\leq\nC\\left\\|\nw\\mathbf1_{\\{|x|&gt;R\\}\\cup\\{w&gt;L\\}}\n\\right\\|_p\n\\|\\psi\\|_{H^1}^2.\n\\]\nThe coefficient tends to zero as \\(R,L\\to\\infty\\), proving that \\(T\\) is compact.</p><p>Finally, fix \\(a&gt;0\\). If the spectral subspace of \\(H_W\\) in\n\\((-\\infty,-a]\\) were infinite-dimensional, it would contain an\n\\(L^2\\)-orthonormal sequence \\((\\psi_j)\\). Semiboundedness and the spectral theorem place this sequence in the form domain with uniformly bounded shifted form norm, hence uniformly bounded \\(H^1\\) norm. A weakly convergent subsequence in \\(H^1\\) has limit zero, because an orthonormal sequence converges weakly to zero in \\(L^2\\).</p><p>Compactness of \\(T\\) would then give \\(\\|T\\psi_j\\|_2\\to0\\), whereas\n\\[\n-a\n\\geq h_W[\\psi_j]\n=\n\\|\\psi_j'\\|_2^2-\\|T\\psi_j\\|_2^2\n\\geq-\\|T\\psi_j\\|_2^2,\n\\]\na contradiction. Every such spectral subspace is therefore finite-dimensional, which proves the assertion about the negative spectrum.</p>"}], "edges": [[2, 1, "cite"], [2, 6, "cite"], [2, 11, "cite"], [3, 2, "cite"], [3, 5, "cite"], [3, 6, "cite"], [3, 9, "cite"], [4, 2, "cite"], [4, 5, "cite"], [5, 2, "cite"], [6, 1, "cite"], [6, 11, "cite"], [7, 11, "cite"], [7, 12, "cite"], [7, 13, "cite"], [7, 15, "cite"], [7, 16, "cite"], [8, 7, "cite"], [8, 10, "cite"], [9, 7, "cite"], [9, 10, "cite"], [10, 7, "cite"], [11, 16, "cite"], [12, 16, "cite"], [13, 12, "cite"], [13, 14, "cite"], [14, 12, "cite"], [15, 12, "cite"], [16, 0, "cite"], [17, 0, "cite"]]});
